I"87<p>Roman Album Kiki’s Delivery Service (Majo no Takkyūbin), issued October 15, 1989</p>

<p>A day in July after the film was completed and waiting its release. An in-person interview at Studio Ghibli with director Miyazaki. What is the theme of Kiki’s Delivery Service? He spoke enthusiastically about this and other topics.</p>

<p>What are the characteristics of adolescence?</p>

<p>—I would like to start by asking what your intentions were in creating Kiki’s Delivery Service.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: The starting point was wanting to film the story of a girl in adolescence. Moreover, she would be one of the ordinary girls who come to Tokyo from the countryside. I wanted to depict the situations faced by such girls in our current society. This is a fictional story set in a fictional land where witches live. But our story also deals with girls who arrive in the big city, get a room and a job—what they face as they settle in. If we could show an appealing girl facing these challenges, we thought viewers would be able to empathize with her.</p>

<p>At the same time, it was also my obstinate way of showing up those who say of my work, “All of the girls you focus on are princesses.” [laughs]</p>

<p>—It does seem that Kiki is, in many ways, a normal girl.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: Yes, the characters in this film are fundamentally different from those in my previous films, films that featured dramatic stories of life hanging in the balance and characters experiencing great hardships. The hallmark of this film is the expression of the many faces of a person. In the presence of her parents, Kiki is childish, but on her own she thinks things over with a serious expression. She may speak roughly and bluntly to a boy her own age, but to her seniors, especially to people important to her, she acts politely. These faces are not calculated but rather come out either naturally or as a result of what she has learned from her parents. My thought was that one characteristic of adolescence is learning to understand the appropriate use of these various facial expressions.</p>

<p>—The main character is a girl who has these various faces she shows to others.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: This is a very natural thing in our everyday lives. If it seems somewhat calculated, it is the unavoidable folly of youth [laughs], something that is common. My aim in making this film was not to express my opinion about each of these faces, but to make this kind of girl happy. [laughs]</p>

<p>Here I do want to note that Kiki goes into a slump when she finds she can’t fly. Kiki will be depressed about other things in the future as well. She may become sad, but she will climb out of that feeling. That’s how I wanted to end the film. I didn’t want it to have a happy ending with her business becoming a success or her becoming the town’s sweetheart. I wanted to give the sense that she will repeatedly become dejected and then regain her cheery vitality … I was careful from the start to avoid making it a career success story.</p>

<p>—That ending, then, wasn’t simply a happy ending?</p>

<p>Miyazaki: I certainly wanted the ending to be a scene viewers would be happy with. I also wanted to show Osono-san’s baby somewhere … And what I thought I needed to do most for Kiki was to have her become friends with girls her own age.</p>

<p>—They are the girls she met in the town—the ones she at first resisted?</p>

<p>Miyazaki: More than resisted, Kiki held a grudge against them. When Kiki was able to throw off her own grudge, she found that the people around her were not really mean-spirited. When she delivered the old woman’s pie, she was given the cold shoulder by the girl, but that happens sometimes when you run a delivery service. It wasn’t a particularly horrible occurrence, but that is what you encounter in your work.</p>

<p>That is how I see it, and Kiki’s wishful thinking was brought up short by it. She was sure she would be thanked … But that isn’t it. She was paid, so she had to deliver the pie. And if, by chance, she meets people through her business, she is fortunate. That is the way work goes … I didn’t delve that deeply into attitudes toward work in this film, though. Each time a deliveryman comes to our door, we don’t say, “You must be tired. Come on in for a cup of tea.” [laughs] We just sign for it, say thank you, and that’s it.</p>

<p>—But it may be different if it were a delivery girl.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: Nah, I don’t think so. That’s why I like what the girl at the party says as she comes to the door. She’s not lying, she’s speaking honestly. She’s thinking, “I told Grandmother I didn’t need it, but still she sent a pie.” This kind of thing happens often in real life. It might have been shocking for Kiki to hear and may have hurt her deeply, but there are many things in this world that we just have to bear.</p>

<p>What is Kiki’s magic?</p>

<p>—How should we understand the magic shown in this film? For example, Kiki’s ability to fly, and when she is unable to fly.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: Why do you think Kiki can fly? Is it because she’s a witch?</p>

<p>—In the movie, they say she has flying in her blood …</p>

<p>Miyazaki: What is blood, though? It’s what we receive from our parents, isn’t it? It’s not something we learn to do. Talent is like that. We all have to pass through a period when we are unconsciously using a talent, while learning to consciously make that power our own. This is what Ursula says. She could paint, but what she thought was her own talent was actually something she inherited. This is the same for everyone. During our twenties, thirties, and forties we work to be able to understand our own abilities and limits, and ultimately find their level. Becoming unable to do something we were once able to do unconsciously means that it is also impossible to grow without becoming conscious of our talents and developing them.</p>

<p>This is why the magic in this film is restricted to Kiki’s type of talent and is distinct from the tradition of true witchcraft. There is every likelihood that she might not be able to fly at some point—the issue is whether we want a reason for her inability to fly.</p>

<p>—It may be because she fought with Tombo.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: But even giving a reason for it doesn’t help clarify the problem. Rather, I think this kind of expression might satisfy the girls watching the film—isn’t it something like, “I could do this until just yesterday, but why did I fall all of a sudden? Why do people’s words sting so much? Why have I lost so much energy?” In our work as animators, too, we often come to a point when we can’t seem to draw anymore. Then we wonder how we were able to draw before and why our skills have seemingly left us. We don’t know why that happens. And reasoning doesn’t help … There are all sorts of examples like that.</p>

<p>It’s not just talent, but our feelings as well. Kiki doesn’t know how to deal with her own feelings. That is what adolescence is about. Even if you don’t mean to pick a fight, when words are exchanged you might use much sharper words than you intended or you might make a stupid comment. You might be thinking it’s better to do this, but react and do the exact opposite. We do all sorts of contrary things …</p>

<p>—And you’re showing that’s what adolescence is?</p>

<p>Miyazaki: Yes, though recently there seem to be people who are still doing this sort of thing in their thirties and forties, even … A person who has established himself or herself in a secure manner should be able to take the same attitude toward anyone wherever he or she goes. Or, we all want to take the same attitude. But in actuality, when watching the shop Kiki jitters her leg nervously when no one is there, and when someone passes by she suddenly smiles … That’s not because she’s sneaky. People have to do many things like this to go out into the world. It’s not about whether these gestures help. What will fulfill us most in those instances? There are obvious things, like gaining confidence from work going well, or earning more money than others and feeling superior with wealthy trappings. What I think becomes most necessary for a girl like Kiki is a place to take refuge.</p>

<p>What are the foolish doings of youth?</p>

<p>—Where was Kiki’s refuge?</p>

<p>Miyazaki: I think it is her getting to know someone who will respect and understand her in her struggle with a life she doesn’t understand well. This is why she asks Ursula if she can visit from time to time. Ursula answers that she will be there during the summer. This place becomes critical for Kiki in her effort to bounce back. The old lady who baked Kiki a cake was a nice acquaintance. But what gave Kiki more pleasure was the first person who visited her room as a friend, not as a landlord. What’s more, Ursula understands Kiki’s anguish in an affirming way. I think these aspects are much more valuable to Kiki than whether her business will succeed.</p>

<p>This is the same for the animators around me. They go out drinking late at night and all say, “Maybe I should call it quits,” or “Maybe I should give up and go back to my hometown.” They too are searching for a place of refuge and trying out different ways of living. But it’s not easy for them to regain their vitality. They end up repeating their feelings—things didn’t work out, can’t work out. We can’t help but think that disappointment is our path in life. Those reading this book will also go on in a similar way. But people often turn out to be helpful when we get to know them. “Hey, that’s the theme of this film!” is what Yoshifumi Kondō told me. [laughs]</p>

<p>—Rather than being a little witch heroine who brings happiness to those around her, Kiki is a normal girl who wants to become happy herself.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: And she is helped by those around her even when she isn’t aware of it. What’s most important for Kiki is not that her business succeed—though of course that is no doubt important—it is how she herself can become acquainted with many different people. While she rides on her broom accompanied by her cat and flies around the sky, she is free. That’s because she’s at a distance from people. But to live in the town, in other words, to train for a profession, requires her to confront herself in an honest way. In terms of Kiki, can she become someone who can comfortably walk around town alone and converse with people without her broom or cat? In our world everyone arms themselves with the latest fashions, reads up on performances in magazines like Pia and uses them to get to the movie theater without asking anyone for directions (because they don’t want to embarrass themselves). That’s why information magazines sell so well. No one wants to be embarrassed (or show their ignorance) so they assiduously rehearse their actions.</p>

<p>I understand this so well because I am very self-conscious. Sometimes I can’t even ask a train station attendant if I am at the correct exit. Sometimes I’m even too embarrassed to stand still and look around to figure out which street I should take in town—I keep trudging along the wrong street and end up nowhere near my destination. I’ve done a lot of foolish things like that in my life … I never thought that I would want to depict this sort of thing in a film, but at my age I’ve come to look back on such things objectively—foolish behavior can actually be quite heartwarming.</p>

<p>—That is the kind of foolish behavior that we all unavoidably engage in.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: I think everyone has this kind of experience; we human beings do many stupid things. So I don’t think we can negate them as being bad or wrong. But they aren’t things we should continually do, either. It would be best if we didn’t have to do them. We’ve all done things that make us want to shout in mortification when we recall them years later in the middle of the night, but we have to go on doing these things. After we become parents, we regret the things we’ve said to our own parents—but then our parents must have thought the same thing when they were young. And we’ll no doubt hear it from our children. I wonder if I will think about these things differently in the future. But this is a grown-up’s impression, and I think making a film like this is due to my having aged …</p>

<p>—You said you didn’t intend to direct a success story.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: Our times are different from the past. In the old days of apprenticeships, daily life was extremely harsh and grueling. Adolescents faced hardship and poverty; they had a drive to graduate to journeyman status as soon as possible; sometimes they just wanted to return home. Nowadays (though not everyone may be like this) there are idiotic parents who say, “Come back if things don’t work out,” even when the “things” involve marriage. [laughs] And, on top of that, they give their children a considerable amount of money. It’s quite different from the training that young people endured in the past. Yet, in terms of psychological issues there are still similarities. That was the idea behind making this film.</p>

<p>There are many young women in our company like this. Most of Kiki’s mannerisms are taken from what I have observed of the behavior of young women animators I trained when they were new hires. So I’m afraid they may recognize themselves. [laughs] It would have been better if I could have sublimated their behavior into humor. But there was the problem of whether that would work as animation … I thought it would be nice for a change to work on a small-scale film about regular life. It’s enough to have made just one film like this. [laughs]</p>

<p>—So, what kind of film will your next one be?</p>

<p>Miyazaki: I want to make a nonsensical film that can be enjoyed on a simple level. That’s how I feel now, though I don’t know what it will be.</p>
